    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Normal faults at Chimney Rock in the San Rafael Swell, Utah are developed in four planar orientations with oppositely dipping fault pairs trending ENE and WNW. ENE faults initially developed by forming deformation bands and associated slip surfaces. Later this system was reactivated by shear localized along the fault slip surfaces producing splay fractures and breccia. WNW faults are dominated by shearing along older joints accompanied by splay fracturing. Deformation band-dominated faults form a dense zone of deformation bands and a related through-going slip surface at offsets as small as 45 cm. Joint-dominated faults are defined by broad damage zones; the density of joints along the fault increases with increasing offset. Juxtaposition of both deformation mechanisms along two well-defined strike sets of faults suggests that within a single rock type and location, a wide variety of fault styles are likely. Recognizing two different mechanisms of faulting provides a basis for distinguishing the deformation history of a fault system composed of multiple sets of faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
